==Old English==

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|ang|bʰruHg}}
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*brūkaną||to enjoy, use}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*bʰruHg-||enjoy}}. Cognate with Old Frisian {{m|ofs|brūka}}, Old High German {{m|goh|brūhhan}} (German {{m|de|brauchen}}), Old Saxon {{m|osx|brūkan}}, Middle Dutch {{m|dum|bruken}} (Dutch {{m|nl|gebruiken}}), Gothic {{m|got|𐌱𐍂𐌿𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽|tr=brūkjan}}. The Indo-European root was also the source of Latin {{m|la|fruor||enjoy}} and {{m|la|frūx||fruit}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈbruːkɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|head=brūcan}}

# to [[enjoy]], to make use of
# to [[digest]] food

